  "body": {
    "id": "prop:fixed-privacy-reduction",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "For fixed \\(\\epsilon\\in(0,1]\\) and \\(q<\\gamma\\),\n\\[\n \\rho_G\\vee\\rho_F=O(R_n^{\\mathrm{NP}})\n\\]\nunconditionally as \\(n\\to\\infty\\), where the implicit constant may depend on the fixed privacy budget.  If Open-ended Question~1 has an affirmative answer, the conditional frontier theorem consequently reduces to\n\\[\n R_{n,\\epsilon}^{\\mathrm{DP}}\\asymp R_n^{\\mathrm{NP}}.\n\\]\nNo unconditional private upper bound is asserted by this proposition.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "epsilon",
      "q",
      "gamma",
      "rhoG",
      "rhoF",
      "RNP",
      "RDP",
      "n"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:two-scale-regime",
      "def:frontier-radii",
      "thm:combined-private-frontier",
      "lem:nonprivate-cate-rate-floor"
    ],
    "justification": "The unconditional radius comparison checks fixed-budget compatibility with the nonprivate rate; the private-risk equivalence remains conditional on the same unresolved full-fiber premise as the frontier theorem.",
    "gap": "This reduction is a consistency check against Kennedy et al., not a separate novelty claim.",
    "consumer": "Users may treat both privacy radii as asymptotically dominated at a fixed budget, but may invoke a matching private upper risk only after the full-fiber lift is established.",
    "proof_tex": "The nonprivate CATE rate floor gives a constant \\(c>0\\) such that\n\\[\n R_n^{\\mathrm{NP}}\n \\ge c\\left\\{n^{-1/(2+d/\\gamma)}\n \\vee n^{-2/(2+d/\\gamma+d/q)}\\right\\}.\n \\tag{38}\n\\]\nFix \\(\\epsilon\\in(0,1]\\), independently of \\(n\\).  The radius definitions and \\(1\\wedge x\\le x\\) give\n\\[\n \\rho_G\\le\\epsilon^{-1/(1+d/\\gamma)}n^{-1/(1+d/\\gamma)},\n \\qquad\n \\rho_F\\le\\epsilon^{-1/(1+d/\\gamma+d/q)}n^{-2/(1+d/\\gamma+d/q)}.\n \\tag{39}\n\\]\nSince \\(q>0\\) and \\(\\gamma>0\\),\n\\[\n \\frac1{1+d/\\gamma}>\\frac1{2+d/\\gamma},\n \\qquad\n \\frac2{1+d/\\gamma+d/q}>\\frac2{2+d/\\gamma+d/q}.\n \\tag{40}\n\\]\nFor \\(n\\ge1\\), (39)--(40) bound each privacy radius by a fixed-budget constant times the corresponding term on the right of (38).  Hence \\(\\rho_G\\vee\\rho_F=O(R_n^{\\mathrm{NP}})\\) unconditionally.  The condition \\(q<\\gamma\\) ensures that this comparison is made on the declared two-scale model class; the exponent inequalities themselves use only positivity.\n\nNow additionally assume an affirmative answer to Open-ended Question~1.  The conditional frontier theorem and the just-proved comparison give\n\\[\n R_{n,\\epsilon}^{\\mathrm{DP}}\n \\asymp R_n^{\\mathrm{NP}}\\vee\\rho_G\\vee\\rho_F\n \\asymp R_n^{\\mathrm{NP}}.\n\\]\nThis last step uses the open-ended premise, whereas the radius comparison does not.",
